Superpermutations of the first N letters, for N = 1 to 7, by four methods:
  every permutation: each permutation written out in turn (the yardstick, N! * N letters)
  unseen only: the permutations from the last, each added where the string lacks it
  greedy: add the fewest letters, a rearrangement of the oldest of the last N, that
          end in a permutation not seen yet
  recursive: each permutation P of N - 1 letters, in the order the string for N - 1
             holds them, made P N P, joined at their overlaps (1! + 2! + ... + N!)

method                N=1    N=2    N=3    N=4    N=5    N=6    N=7  mean ratio
every permutation       1      4     18     96    600   4320  35280       1.000
unseen only             1      4     15     64    325   1956  13699       0.657
greedy                  1      3      9     35    164    932   6247       0.392
recursive               1      3      9     33    153    873   5913       0.379

Each string holds all N! permutations. Best: recursive.
The shortest possible lengths are known up to N = 5: 1, 3, 9, 33, 153 (OEIS A180632).

The strings for N = 3:
every permutation ABCACBBACBCACABCBA
unseen only       CBACABBCAACBABC
greedy            ABCABACBA
recursive         ABCABACBA
